// ============================================================================
// hipaa_164_530.dl — §164.530(c): Administrative requirements — safeguards
// ============================================================================
// §164.530(c)(1) requires covered entities to have appropriate administrative,
// technical, and physical safeguards in place to protect PHI. §164.530(c)(2)
// requires reasonably safeguarding PHI from any intentional or unintentional
// use/disclosure that violates the subpart, and limiting incidental uses and
// disclosures made pursuant to an otherwise permitted or required use.
//
// A covered entity is presumed to have implemented the required safeguards
// unless the scenario explicitly states non-compliance (lacks_reasonable_safeguards
// below) -- this is a real negation, not just documented intent: the presumption
// has a genuine escape hatch via lacks_reasonable_safeguards, a real,
// LLM-assertable negative fact.
//
// permitted_by_164_530_c is declared in hipaa_stubs.dl and is required by
// permitted_by_164_502_a_1_iii (incident-to-use pathway).
// ============================================================================

// lacks_reasonable_safeguards: oracle -- narrative evidence that the covered
// entity does NOT have administrative, technical, or physical safeguards in
// place, or failed to reasonably limit this specific incidental disclosure
// (§164.530(c)(1)/(c)(2)). Negated below: default is presumed-compliant
// (per the comment above), but this predicate gives that presumption a real
// escape hatch instead of being unconditional.
.decl lacks_reasonable_safeguards(p1: Principal)

permitted_by_164_530_c(ARGS) :-
    is_covered_entity(p1),
    is_phi(t),
    msg_contains(m, q, t),
    disclosure_attempted(ARGS),
    !lacks_reasonable_safeguards(p1).
